.TH CCTXPACK 1 "2026-10-17" "cctxpack 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cctxpack \- pack a repository into a single token-budgeted LLM context bundle
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cctxpack
.RI [ pack ]
.RI [ paths ...]
.br
.B cctxpack count
.RI [ paths ...]
.br
.B cctxpack tree
.RI [ path ]
.br
.B cctxpack diff
.RB [ \-\-staged ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cctxpack
collects source files from a directory or git work tree and renders them as a
single bundle suitable for pasting into an LLM. Discovery respects
.BR .gitignore ;
inside a work tree untracked-but-not-ignored files are included.
.PP
Files are ordered deterministically (byte-wise), then fitted to an optional
token budget. Per-file token counts are
.IR estimates ,
not exact tokenizer counts, and are labelled as such in every output format.
.PP
Every bundle begins with a provenance
.IR manifest :
the commit SHA of the work tree (or
.BR unversioned ),
the number of files included, their content bytes and estimated tokens, and a
digest identifying the exact file set and its order. The manifest contains no
timestamp, so two runs over an unchanged tree produce byte-identical output.
Its own cost is charged against
.BR \-\-budget
and included in the reported total.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B pack
Pack files into a bundle. This is the default when no command is given.
.TP
.B count
Print a per-file bytes/lines/tokens table instead of a bundle.
.TP
.B tree
Render the directory tree only.
.TP
.B diff
Pack files changed since HEAD. By default this spans staged, unstaged and
untracked changes, which is what someone asking for a review wants; use
.B \-\-staged
for the index alone. Deleted paths are never included, because there is no
content to pack.
.TP
.B diff \-\-patch
Emit the unified diff itself instead of whole files. Use this when the consumer
has no earlier copy of the file to compare against. Untracked files have no diff
representation and so never appear.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-budget N
Token budget (estimated). Auto-enables the report.
.TP
.BI \-\-format " md|xml|json|txt"
Output format. Default
.BR md .
.TP
.BI \-\-include " GLOB"
Include only paths matching GLOB. Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude " GLOB"
Exclude paths matching GLOB. Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-file\-tokens " N"
Truncate files above N estimated tokens.
.TP
.BI \-\-sort " name|mtime|size"
Ordering. Default
.BR name .
.TP
.BI \-\-query " TEXT"
Re-rank candidates by ASCII case-insensitive keyword overlap in the path.
Basename matches count double. Lexical, not semantic.
.TP
.B \-\-clipboard
Copy the bundle to the clipboard (Windows only). Cannot be combined with
.BR \-o .
.TP
.B \-\-staged
With
.BR diff ,
use staged changes only.
.TP
.B \-\-patch
With
.BR diff ,
emit the unified diff instead of whole files. The bundle then contains a single
section named
.BR changes.diff .
.TP
.BR \-\-tree ", " \-\-no\-tree
Include or omit the directory tree. On by default, off for
.BR diff .
.TP
.BI \-o " PATH"
Write to PATH instead of stdout.
.TP
.B \-\-report
Force the fitting report.
.TP
.B \-\-config
Print the resolved configuration and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-find " PATTERN"
Fixed-string, line-oriented search. Prints
.I path:line: text
for every matching line and exits instead of packing. Honours path arguments,
.B \-\-include
and
.BR \-\-exclude ,
and skips binary files. Case-sensitive
.B grep \-F
semantics: no regular expressions. With
.BR \-\-report ,
the count of matches and files is summarised on stderr.
.B \-o
writes the matches to a file rather than stdout. Exits 4 if nothing matched.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Show usage.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Show version.
.SH OUTLINE
.B \-\-outline
sends a file's
.IR declarations " instead of its"
.IR body :
its imports and signatures, each with the line number it came from. That is
enough to know what a repository contains without paying for how it is
implemented.
.PP
The flag has two modes, selected by whether
.B \-\-budget
is also given:
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-\-outline
alone
.PP
Every parseable file is sent as declarations. Files the scanner refuses, and
binaries, are sent whole rather than dropped, so nothing that a plain pack
would have included goes missing.
.IP \(bu 2
.BI "\-\-outline " "\-\-budget " N
.PP
.IR Progressive .
One pass in relevance order: a file takes its whole body if that fits, otherwise
its outline if that fits, otherwise it is dropped and listed in the report. The
most relevant files arrive complete and the rest of the repository still arrives
at all, which is the point. The budget remains a hard cap either way.
.PP
Files that the scanner cannot account for are sent
.IR whole ,
not outlined. Unbalanced braces, an extension outside the supported set, or a
substantial file yielding nothing recognisable all cause a refusal. A wrong
outline is worse than none, because a consumer cannot tell them apart. Markdown
and other prose are not outlined for the same reason: cctxpack does not invent
structure it cannot see.
.PP
With
.BR \-\-format " " json
the outline is a structured array rather than rendered text: an object per
declaration, carrying a
.B line
number and the
.B decl
text. A consumer can select from that array programmatically;
it cannot select from prose.
.B count \-\-outline
reports what would actually be sent, using the same code path as
.BR pack .
.SH TRUNCATION
Files are read up to
.B 4 MiB
.RB ( READ_CAP_DEFAULT ).
This is a memory bound over whatever path the user pointed at, not a tuning
knob, and it is deliberately not configurable: an escape hatch would trade a
bounded failure for an out-of-memory one. Anything larger is read up to the cap
and the rest is dropped.
.PP
.B \-\-max\-file\-tokens N
applies on top of that cap, not instead of it.
.PP
Whenever content is dropped, cctxpack says so rather than leaving it to be
inferred.
.B count
adds a note column to its table, and in
.B \-\-format json
adds
.B truncated
plus
.BR truncation ,
which is
.B read\-cap
or
.BR token\-cap .
The two are distinguished because they have opposite remedies: the first is a
limit you cannot lift with a flag, the second is a limit you set. A token cap
drops no bytes, so
.B bytes_packed
appears only for a read cap.
.PP
The same fields
.RB ( truncated ", " bytes_packed )
appear in the bundle manifest, and
.B \-\-report
names the reasons:
.PP
.RS
cctxpack: included 12 files (3 truncated: 1 at read cap, 2 at token cap), excluded 5
.RE
.PP
Note that
.B bytes
in every output means
.IR "bytes on disk" ,
while
.B lines
and
.B tokens
describe what was actually read. Those only describe the same content when
nothing was dropped, which is why
.B bytes_packed
exists.
.SH REDACTION
.B \-\-redact
replaces credential-shaped substrings with
.BI [REDACTED: kind ]
markers. It is
.BR "opt-in" :
without the flag cctxpack emits exactly the bytes on disk, because fidelity is
the tool's core promise and silently rewriting content would break it.
.PP
What is replaced:
.IP \(bu 2
Whole PEM private key blocks, from
.B BEGIN
through the matching
.BR "END" ;
.IP \(bu 2
Vendor token shapes:
.BR AKIA… ,
.BR ghp_ / gho_ / ghs_ / ghr_ ,
.BR github_pat_ ,
.BR AIza… ,
.BR xox… ,
.BR sk_live_ ,
and JWTs;
.IP \(bu 2
Values assigned to unambiguously secret names
.RB ( password ,
.BR secret ,
.BR api_key ,
.BR access_token ,
.BR client_secret ,
and similar), of at least 6 bytes.
.PP
What is deliberately
.B not
replaced:
.IP \(bu 2
Anything matched by an entropy heuristic. This is the single most important
limitation: length-and-alphabet scoring mislabels SHA digests, base64 assets,
lockfile integrity hashes and minified bundles far more often than it catches a
real key, and misses keys that live in environment variables rather than in
source.
.IP \(bu 2
Placeholders
.RB ( your… ,
.BR ${VAR} ,
.BR <…> ,
.BR changeme ,
and similar) and derived values such as
.BR password_hash .
.IP \(bu 2
Secret-shaped names embedded in a longer identifier, so
.B password_hash
is not read as
.BR password .
.PP
Two guarantees worth relying on:
.IP \(bu 2
Only the secret
.I span
is replaced, so
.B db_password = "<the-secret>"
becomes
.B db_password = "<the-secret>"
and still parses.
.IP \(bu 2
The
.I line count
is preserved exactly, including for multi-line key blocks, so line numbers a
consumer was given remain valid.
.PP
The number of replacements appears in the bundle manifest (as
.B redactions
only when non-zero) and on stderr as
.RB \(lq cctxpack: "redacted N credential(s)" \(rq.
A consumer can therefore tell a scrubbed file from an empty one.
.B \-\-redact
is rejected with
.B tree
(exit 2), which never reads file content and would otherwise silently do
nothing.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The nearest
.B .cctxpack.json
at or above the current directory is merged in, bounded by the git work-tree
root (current directory only outside a work tree). Recognised keys:
.BR budget ", " format ", " include ", " exclude ", " max_file_tokens ", " tree .
Command-line flags always win. An invalid or wrongly-typed configuration is a
usage error.
.PP
A
.B .cctxpackignore
file in the repository root is applied after
.B .gitignore
and after
.BR \-\-exclude :
one glob per line,
.B #
starts a comment, blank lines are skipped, surrounding whitespace is trimmed. It
drops files from the bundle without untracking them from git. A missing file is
not an error.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CTX_VOCAB_DIR
Reserved for an optional partial vocabulary directory. Not used by this build.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
I/O, out-of-memory, or read failure.
.TP
.B 2
Usage or configuration error, including unknown flags, bad values, invalid
JSON, and
.B diff
outside a work tree.
.TP
.B 3
Nothing fits the budget.
.TP
.B 4
.B \-\-find
found no match. The search itself succeeded; only the result set was empty.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Pack the current repository:
.PP
.RS
cctxpack . \-o context.md
.RE
.PP
Pack a subsystem within a token budget:
.PP
.RS
cctxpack src/parser \-\-budget 8000
.RE
.PP
Pack only what changed, for review:
.PP
.RS
cctxpack diff \-\-staged \-o review.md
.RE
.PP
Find files relevant to a question first:
.PP
.RS
cctxpack . \-\-query "http retry" \-\-budget 6000
.RE
.SH NOTES
Token counts are produced by a script-aware heuristic estimator fitted against
tiktoken. Aggregate error over a repository is roughly +10% to +25%; per-file
error can reach \-26%. See
.B docs/design-document.md
section 4.6 in the source distribution for the calibration record.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git-ls-files (1),
.BR tar (1)
